
1. LLM (Large Language Model)

Layman’s Explanation
Imagine a person who has read millions of books, websites, articles, and documents. When you ask a question, they predict the next most likely words to answer you.
That’s an LLM.

Examples:

* ChatGPT
* GPT-4o
* Claude
* Gemini
* Llama

Interview Answer:
An LLM is a deep learning model trained on massive text datasets to understand and generate human-like language.

⸻

2. Prompt

Layman’s Explanation
A prompt is simply the instruction you give the AI.

Like asking a chef:

“Make me spicy pasta.”

The chef’s cooking depends on your instruction.

Example:

Summarize this PDF.

That’s the prompt.

⸻

3. Prompt Engineering

Layman’s Explanation

It’s the art of asking better questions to get better answers.

Bad Prompt:

Tell me about AWS.

Good Prompt:

Explain AWS Glue for beginners in less than 100 words with one real-world example.

The second prompt gives much better results.

⸻

4. Token

Layman’s Explanation

AI doesn’t read sentences.

It breaks everything into small pieces called tokens.

Example:

I love cricket.

May become

"I"
"love"
"cricket"
"."

Think of tokens as Lego blocks that AI uses to build sentences.

⸻

5. Context Window

Layman’s Explanation

Think of it as the AI’s short-term memory.

If you keep talking for hours, eventually the AI starts forgetting the earliest parts because its memory has a limit.

That limit is called the context window.

⸻

6. Temperature

Layman’s Explanation

It controls how creative the AI is.

Low Temperature (0)

Like a calculator.

Always gives nearly the same answer.

High Temperature (1)

Like a creative writer.

Answers become more varied and imaginative.

⸻

7. Embeddings

One of the most asked interview questions.

Layman’s Explanation

Imagine Google Maps.

Every city has coordinates.

Similarly, AI converts every sentence into coordinates (a vector) based on its meaning.

Example:

Dog

and

Puppy

will have nearby coordinates because they mean similar things.

Example:

Apple company

and

Microsoft

will also be closer than

Apple

and

Banana

depending on the context.

Embeddings let AI compare meanings instead of exact words.

⸻

8. Vector

Layman’s Explanation

A vector is simply a long list of numbers.

Example

[0.12, -0.45, 0.98, ....]

Humans see

Insurance Policy

AI sees

[0.21,0.43,-0.89....]

⸻

9. Vector Database

Layman’s Explanation

Think of a huge library.

Instead of arranging books alphabetically, it arranges them by meaning.

When you ask

Leave Policy

it finds documents that mean the same thing, even if they don’t contain the exact words.

Examples

Azure AI Search

Pinecone

FAISS

Milvus

Chroma

⸻

10. Similarity Search

Layman’s Explanation

Suppose you search

Car

Instead of finding only “Car”

It also finds

Vehicle
Automobile
SUV
Sedan

because they have similar meanings.

⸻

11. Cosine Similarity

Layman’s Explanation

Imagine two arrows.

If both point in almost the same direction,

they’re similar.

If one points left and another right,

they’re very different.

Cosine Similarity measures how close two meanings are.

⸻

12. Chunking

Very common interview question.

Layman’s Explanation

Suppose you have a 500-page PDF.

Instead of reading the whole book every time,

you cut it into smaller pages.

Those small pieces are called chunks.

⸻

13. Chunk Overlap

Layman’s Explanation

Suppose Page 10 ends with

Customer can claim...

and Page 11 starts with

...within 30 days.

Without overlap,

AI loses the sentence.

So we repeat a few lines in the next chunk.

That’s chunk overlap.

⸻

14. RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation)

Layman’s Explanation

Imagine an open-book exam.

Instead of answering from memory,

you first open the book,

find the relevant page,

read it,

then answer.

That’s exactly what RAG does.

⸻

15. Retriever

Layman’s Explanation

Like a librarian.

You ask

Where is the leave policy?

The librarian finds the correct book.

The retriever finds the correct document chunk.

⸻

16. Generator

Layman’s Explanation

After the librarian gives the book,

the teacher explains it.

The teacher is the Generator (GPT).

⸻

17. Hallucination

Layman’s Explanation

When AI confidently gives a wrong answer.

Example

AI says

India has 35 states.

It sounds confident,

but it’s incorrect.

That’s hallucination.

⸻

18. Grounding

Layman’s Explanation

Instead of guessing,

AI answers only from trusted documents.

Like an employee saying,

“According to the HR policy…”

That’s grounding.

⸻

19. Fine Tuning

Layman’s Explanation

Imagine teaching a chef a new cuisine permanently.

After training,

the chef changes how they cook.

That’s fine-tuning.

⸻

20. RAG vs Fine-Tuning

RAG

* Retrieves latest documents.
* No retraining.
* Best for frequently changing information like company policies.

Fine-Tuning

* Changes the model’s behavior.
* Requires retraining.
* Best for domain-specific writing style or task adaptation.

⸻

21. LangChain

Layman’s Explanation

Think of LangChain as the project manager.

GPT only answers questions.

LangChain coordinates everything:

* Reads PDFs
* Splits documents
* Creates embeddings
* Stores vectors
* Searches documents
* Calls GPT
* Returns the answer

⸻

22. Agent

Layman’s Explanation

Instead of only answering,

AI can decide what to do.

Example:

Find today's sales.
↓
Open SQL Database
↓
Run Query
↓
Create Graph
↓
Summarize
↓
Email Manager

This decision-making workflow is an AI Agent.

⸻

23. Tool

Layman’s Explanation

A tool is something the AI can use besides its own knowledge.

Examples:

* SQL Database
* Calculator
* Weather API
* SharePoint
* Azure Blob Storage
* REST API

The AI decides when to use these tools.

⸻

24. Guardrails

Layman’s Explanation

Think of them as safety rules.

Just as a car has seat belts and speed limits, GenAI systems have guardrails to prevent harmful or incorrect behavior.

Examples:

* Block abusive content.
* Prevent prompt injection.
* Mask sensitive information (PII).
* Restrict responses to approved company data.
* Ensure answers stay grounded in retrieved documents.

⸻

25. Azure OpenAI

Layman’s Explanation

It’s OpenAI’s models (like GPT-4o) offered through Microsoft’s Azure cloud, with enterprise features such as security, private networking, identity management, compliance, and monitoring.

